N:HOW READ (2026-11-11): No source plate exists because this is a new composition; this is the compositional audit. Every bar totals four eighths (2/4). Bar 1 G4 G4 quarters; bar 2 A4 A4 eighths, G4 quarter; bar 3 E4 E4 G4 G4 eighths; bar 4 A4 quarter, quarter rest; bar 5 A4 A4 G4 G4 eighths; bar 6 E4 E4 eighths, D4 quarter; bar 7 rest; bar 8 E4 E4 eighths, G4 quarter. Pitch set D-E-G-A, range D4-A4, largest interval a minor third. Retitled from "Pata Steps" (the reference song's word). The earlier draft's words did not fit stress ("Groove, groove, lis-ten" on two notes) and its cues sat on sung bars; now the freeze and the partner answer each have their own rest. The analyzer reads level 2 (pentatonic, rests); its Ontario line names a I-IV-V chord progression (Grade 6) and low so and la (Grade 3) as notation concepts, but the chords are for the teacher or Band and SK children learn by rote; only beat, direction and partner response are claimed. Originality: do-re and la-do patterns are common property; checked against "Bounce High, Bounce Low", "Hot Cross Buns", "Ring Around the Rosie", "The Hokey Pokey" and "Head, Shoulders, Knees and Toes", with no shared opening. "Pata Pata" is not quoted in melody, rhythm or words.
N:UNCERTAIN: Whether SK partners can answer within one bar (about 1.25 seconds at quarter = 96) or need the teacher to slow down, whether the turn and freeze are safe in a crowded room, and how a South African educator would want Makeba's song and its authorship introduced. A classroom trial, a South African music educator, a movement/accessibility reviewer and a second score reader must settle these.
